  "description": "Faithful replication with the original photographs: eight photographs of two similar rooms in a hotel in Huangshan, China, one on the 2nd and one on the 4th floor, taken between 3 and 4 pm from 1.0 m in front of the window with a 13 mm wide-angle lens, with the window clear or covered with a bronze, blue or red tinted film. Participants give their gender and age, then rate each photograph on the paper's 13 questions about the room's lighting, visual capacity and the view through the window, each on a 5-point bipolar scale from 1 (most negative) to 5 (most positive), as printed in the paper's Table 1. As in the paper, each participant gets one of three colour orders: clear-bronze-blue-red, red-clear-bronze-blue or blue-clear-red-bronze. Rebuilt from Yu, Wang & Liang (2024), Exploration on Effects of Tinted Glazing on Outdoor Views and Visual Experience Inside Hotel, Buildings 14(6): 1799, with the photographs from its Figures 2 and 3 and the questions from Table 1 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: C2, BZ2, BL2, R2 (2nd floor) and C4, BZ4, BL4, R4 (4th floor), C = clear, BZ = bronze, BL = blue, R = red; the colour order each participant got is in sequence; answers are in rating, keyed by image id, with item codes q1-q13 in the order of Table 1; each image's tint and floor and each item's anchors are stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: Friedman's ANOVA across the eight window conditions for each question, then Wilcoxon signed-rank tests between pairs of conditions with Bonferroni-Holm correction. The paper (45 online respondents aged 18-50, 23 women and 22 men, recruited through the investigators' network at the end of 2022) found no significant differences between the same colour on the two floors; comfort fell in the order clear > bronze > blue > red, bronze was as pleasant and acceptable as clear, the red window hurt visual capacity most, followed by blue, and all tints disturbed the view outside. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper does not say in what order the two floors were shown; here the 2nd-floor photographs come first, then the 4th-floor ones, each in the participant's colour order; (2) the low anchor of question 10 is printed as 'Very full' in the paper, which appears to be an error; here it reads 'Very unpleasant'; (3) the instruction text, the information sheet and the wording of the gender and age questions are ours, and the gender question adds an 'other or prefer not to say' option; (4) anyone can take part; (5) the photographs are the figure panels cropped to remove the panel letters (about 670-680 px wide), which trims a narrow strip at the left of each photograph.",
